  "whyItWorks": "Each chord gets its own shape: wide leaps for {{chord 1}}, a climbing diminished arpeggio for {{chord 2}}. The moves that carry the cadence are both half steps, a half-step approach into the 3rd of the V and the b9 down to the 5th of the i. Half steps pull the hardest, so the line sounds inevitable even though it leaps a lot.",
  "fingeringNote": "Eighth position in the lesson's original key, one finger per fret, starting with the ring finger on the high E string. Bar 1 skips a string twice, so pick it slowly at first. In bar 2 the pinky reaches one fret above the position three times, once on the D string and twice on the B string; let the hand lean up the neck for those notes, then come back for the landing note under the index finger.",
  "practiceTask": "Play the lick over bars 5 to 7 of the weekly standard, then over bars 13 to 15, which have the same chords. Practise bar 1 alone and slowly until the string skips sound even, then join it to bar 2.",
  "variation": {
    "description": "The last two notes of bar 2 swap places, so the 3rd of {{chord 2}} ({{note 2.8}}) comes last and rises a half step to the root of {{chord 3}} ({{note 3.1}}). In a minor key, a V7b9 has two strong half-step pulls: the b9 falls to the 5th, as in the lick, and the 3rd rises to the root, as here.",
